Setting. L is a totally ordered set with its canonical (order) topology. It is the one-dimensional model in Grothendieck's chapter 'Réseaux via découpages', where networks are built by cutting. The previous page defines:
- tronçons (intervals ]a,b[, ]a,b], [a,b[, L_{<a}, L_{>a}, L, ...);
- pseudo-tronçons (ps. tr.): intervals or singletons, i.e. a class of convex subsets;
- their boundary ∂T;
- the condition 'position relative modérée/modulaire': ∂T ∪ ∂T' totally ordered.

This page does four things.

(1) Proposition. If T and T' are pseudo-intervals in moderate relative position, then T∩T' is empty or a pseudo-interval, and ∂(T∩T') ⊂ ∂T ∪ ∂T'. This is called 'immédiat'. It is the obvious fact that an intersection of convex sets is convex, and that its endpoints come from the endpoints of the factors.

(2) An order on pseudo-intervals, 'not inclusion' (the margin stresses this). T < T' means: moderate position, T∩T' = ∅, and t < t' for all t∈T, t'∈T'. This is the usual 'lies entirely to the left of' order on disjoint convex pieces. It is transitive (and strict).

(3) If T < T', then:
- ∂T has a greatest element a, with T ⊂ L_{≤a};
- ∂T' has a least element b, with T' ⊂ L_{≥b};
- a ≤ b.
The 'position relative de raccordement' (junction, the abutting case) is: a = b and exactly one of a∈T, b∈T'. Exclusivity holds because T∩T' = ∅. Example: [c,a] and ]a,d].

(4) A strengthened relation: T ≪ T' iff T < T' in proper (non-junction) position. This is claimed equivalent to: there exists x∈L with T ⊂ L_{<x} and T' ⊂ L_{>x}, i.e. a separating point.

The purpose is to prepare the next page. There, for non-abutting T < T', Grothendieck builds the unique 'interstitial' pseudo-interval S with T < S < T', where S abuts both and ∂S = {a,b}. He then defines left and right interstitials of a bounded transversal. All of this is the combinatorics of decomposing a line (one-dimensional cut-up space) into consecutive pieces. It is a building block for the 'découpages' of networks.
